# ─── ili Dashboard Configuration ───────────────────────────────────────
# Copy this file to .env and customize for your environment.
# Most settings have sensible defaults for containerized deployment.

# ─── Where ili listens ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Host port for the web interface. Default 8080 — ports below 1024 cannot be
# bound by a rootless Docker/Podman user.
# ILI_PORT=8080

# ─── Own LAN address (optional, docker-compose.lan.yml) ─────────────────
# Give the web container its own IP in your LAN via macvlan. Static address:
# Docker has no DHCP client for containers — choose an IP outside the router's
# DHCP range. Linux + wired interface only; the ili machine itself cannot reach
# this address (kernel restriction), every other device can. Needs a compose
# that interpolates ${VAR} (see the podman-compose note in QUICKSTART.md).
# LAN_PARENT=eth0
# LAN_SUBNET=192.168.1.0/24
# LAN_GATEWAY=192.168.1.1
# LAN_IP=192.168.1.250
#
# Image version when installing from the registry (ghcr.io/toa1984/ili*).
# Default "latest"; pin a release to stay on it across `docker compose pull`.
# ILI_VERSION=0.1.0

# ─── GitHub feedback channel (optional) ─────────────────────────────────
# Public client id of the GitHub App "ili" — enables Settings → "Mit GitHub
# anmelden" (device flow, your own GitHub account, no secret involved).
# Leave unset to keep the login disabled; issue deep links work without it.
# ILI_GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID=Iv23.xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# ─── Core Directories (Container paths — do NOT change) ───────────────────
# These point to locations inside the container and should remain as-is.
DASHBOARD_DIR=/app
BOARDS_DIR=/app/boards
DASHBOARD_HOST=0.0.0.0
DASHBOARD_PORT=8798

# ─── Domain Configuration (REQUIRED for browser access) ──────────────────
# The domain where ili will be accessible in your network/environment.
# Default: home.arpa (suitable for home networks, requires no DNS).
# For external access or custom domain: set your actual domain name.
DASHBOARD_DOMAIN=home.arpa
# DASHBOARD_DOMAIN=ili.example.com  # Uncomment and customize

# ─── Server URL (Optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Full URL for external sharing/links. If not set, defaults to http://localhost.
# SERVER_URL=http://localhost:8798

# ─── Updates ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Release channel used by the update check and ili-update.sh:
#   stable = tagged releases only (default)
#   beta   = also pre-releases (vX.Y.Z-beta.N)
# ILI_UPDATE_CHANNEL=stable
#
# Disable the periodic GitHub update check entirely (privacy: no outbound
# requests to api.github.com). Default: on.
# ILI_UPDATE_CHECK=off

# ─── Database for your projects (PostgreSQL, starts by default) ──────────
# The stack ships a PostgreSQL container as a building block for the projects
# you build in the project terminal — ili's own boards still stay JSON files
# in the volumes, that part is unchanged. `api` does use it for one thing of
# its own: it mirrors its WARNING/ERROR log lines into a `logs` table so
# bugs.html has real data (see docs/LOGGING.md); without `db` that just falls
# back to stdout-only logging, nothing breaks. If you never need a database,
# remove the `db` service from docker-compose.yml.
#
# Reach it from code running in the project terminal:
#     host db · port 5432 · user/password/database as set below
#     postgresql://ili:CHANGE-ME@db:5432/ili
#
# The port is NOT published to the host — the database is only reachable inside
# the compose network. To attach a database tool, uncomment the `ports:` block
# in the `db` service (bound to 127.0.0.1).
#
# Project containers started the SANDBOX way (docker-compose.sandbox.yml,
# Docker-in-Docker) run in their own network and can NOT reach `db`.
#
# CHANGE THE PASSWORD before you put anything real in here.
POSTGRES_USER=ili
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=CHANGE-ME
POSTGRES_DB=ili
#
# How long api's own log lines stay in the `logs` table before the hourly
# cleanup deletes them (days). Without a limit the db-data volume would grow
# unbounded.
# LOG_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# ─── KI Integration (Optional — not required for basic use) ──────────────
# Local Ollama for experimental KI features.
# Leave unset to disable KI features.
# OLLAMA_URL=http://localhost:11434

# ─── Claude (Optional — not required for basic use) ──────────────────────
# Two ways to give ili access to Claude. One of them is REQUIRED for a working
# install: project preparation (CLAUDE.md, tags, idea cards), the board assistant
# and the Kanban automat all run through the Claude bridge inside the terminal
# container. Without a sign-in every new project stays an empty template (the
# board then shows a red «KI-Vorbereitung fehlgeschlagen» card). Alternatively
# sign in interactively in the project terminal on first open (see below).
#
# 1) Anthropic Console API key — pay per use, no login needed anywhere:
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-api03-...
#
# 2) Long-lived token for a Claude subscription. Generate it once on a machine
#    that has a browser (`claude setup-token`, valid one year) and paste it here:
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=...

# ─── Project Terminal + Claude bridge + Kanban automat ───────────────────
# A browser terminal with one tmux session per board and Claude Code inside —
# and, on the same image, the Claude bridge (port 8950, used by api) and the
# Kanban automat (service `automat`). Part of the standard start since v0.1.7;
# note that a browser terminal is full shell access:
#     docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.terminal.yml up -d
#
# With the next line active, plain `docker compose` commands include the terminal
# (no need to repeat both -f flags). Comment it out for a stack without terminal,
# bridge and automat — new projects then stay empty templates. Docker Compose only:
# podman-compose ignores COMPOSE_FILE from this file, there you have to
# `export COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.terminal.yml` in your shell.
# Windows separates the files with `;` by default — pin the separator so the same
# line works on Linux, macOS and Windows (Docker Compose reads this from .env).
COMPOSE_PATH_SEPARATOR=:
COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.terminal.yml
#
# The /projterm/ route is always password protected. Leave TERMINAL_PASSWORD
# empty and one is generated on every start and printed to the web container's
# log (docker compose logs web | grep ili-setup). Set it here to keep
# a stable password.
# TERMINAL_USER=ili
# TERMINAL_PASSWORD=
#
# Where the terminal keeps project code. Default: ./projects, one subfolder per
# board. Point it at your own code directory to work on existing projects.
# Project folder: ./projects next to the compose files (edit the three volume lines in
# docker-compose.terminal.yml to use another directory)

# ─── SSH access to the project terminal (Optional, off) ──────────────────
# A real SSH login into the terminal container — for scripted access, scp/rsync
# and editors that work over SSH, instead of the browser terminal whose password
# is regenerated on every start.
#
# Two conditions, both required — there is no way to switch this on by accident:
#   1) put your PUBLIC key next to the compose files:
#        mkdir -p ssh && cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub >> ssh/authorized_keys
#      (no key pair yet?  ssh-keygen -t ed25519)
#   2) start with the overlay:
#        docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.terminal.yml \
#                       -f docker-compose.ssh.yml up -d
# Then:  ssh -p 2222 ili@127.0.0.1
#
# Password login and root login are switched off — the key is the only way in.
#
# ⚠️ This is a shell in the terminal container, with your project files and
# passwordless sudo. Combined with docker-compose.hostdocker.yml (Docker socket)
# it is effectively root on the host machine. Whoever holds the PRIVATE key holds
# those rights. That is why it listens on 127.0.0.1 only.
#
# Host port. Not 22: that would collide with an sshd on the host and catch every
# scanner knocking on the default door.
# SSH_PORT=2222
#
# Bind address. 127.0.0.1 = this machine only (default). Set 0.0.0.0 to reach it
# from your LAN — a deliberate decision, not a default. Never expose this port to
# the open internet.
# SSH_BIND=127.0.0.1
#
# Folder holding authorized_keys. Default ./ssh next to the compose files.
# SSH_KEYS_DIR=./ssh

# ─── Project Container Support (Optional) ────────────────────────────────────
# From within a project terminal, you can build and run Docker containers. Choose one:
# (Status, the matching commands and a restart-free third way — a remote daemon over
#  TCP — are on the settings page: KI-Settings -> Docker.)
#
# 1) SANDBOX (Recommended) — isolated Docker-in-Docker, never touches your host.
#    Requires: privileged: true — always fine on Docker; on rootless Podman it
#    depends on the host (worked on ours, but option 2 is the safe route there)
#    Start: docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.terminal.yml \
#                          -f docker-compose.sandbox.yml up -d
#    Ports: the sandbox itself publishes nothing. A separate `gateway` container
#    reserves the range on the host and forwards it 1:1 (host port N -> sandbox:N).
#    Bind your project container to a port in that range and reach it at host:N.
#    Default 8100–8119; each port costs one nginx block plus one host mapping,
#    so widen it deliberately (upper limit 200 ports):
# SANDBOX_PORT_FROM=8100
# SANDBOX_PORT_TO=8119
#
# 2) SOCKET MOUNT — direct host Docker/Podman socket. ⚠️ = root access to host!
#    Only on a machine dedicated to ili. Project containers become plain host
#    containers, so `-p 8100:3000` already publishes a host port — no gateway here.
#    Requires: Docker or Podman
#    Start: docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.terminal.yml \
#                          -f docker-compose.hostdocker.yml up -d
#    Set BOTH of these if using socket mount:
#      Docker (Linux):  /var/run/docker.sock
#      Docker (Windows): /var/run/docker.sock (mapped via WSL2)
#      Podman rootless: /run/user/$(id -u)/podman/podman.sock
#                       needs: systemctl --user enable --now podman.socket
#      Podman rootful:  /run/podman/podman.sock
# DOCKER_SOCKET=/var/run/docker.sock
#    Directory that HOLDS the projects folder (no trailing /projects — the compose
#    file appends it). Project containers are started by the host daemon, so their
#    bind mounts are host paths.
#      Linux:   PROJECTS_HOST_DIR=/home/you/ili
#      Windows: PROJECTS_HOST_DIR=C:\Users\your_username\ili
#               (ili will append \projects automatically)
# PROJECTS_HOST_DIR=/home/you/ili
#
#    ⚠️ podman-compose 1.0.3 (Debian 12) does not interpolate ${VAR} at all — with
#    that version, put the paths straight into docker-compose.hostdocker.yml.
#
# If neither is set, the terminal works normally but `docker` CLI commands will fail.

# Kanban automat tuning (read via env_file):
# AUTOMAT_INTERVAL=300              # seconds between ticks
# AUTOMAT_DEFAULT_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5
